/* Every length is a whole number of pixels. RmlUi snaps glyph quads and geometry translations to
   the pixel grid, so fractional or scaled lengths stay crisp but drift up to a pixel from where
   the layout wanted them. Nothing here is scaled: the bar is drawn small rather than drawn large
   and shrunk, which is what keeps the 12px text readable. */

/* The document fills the viewport so layouts can use the whole screen, but nothing in it claims the
   pointer until an element opts back in. That is what makes the transparent area click-through: the
   host application keeps every mouse message the UI has no element under.

   The bar is centred on the left edge by making the document a flex container, not by `top: 50%`
   plus a translate: the bar's height is content-driven, and a percentage against an auto height
   resolves to zero in RmlUi -- so the translate would do nothing and the bar would sit at the top. */
body {
	font-family: Segoe UI;
	font-size: 14px;
	color: #ffffff;
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	pointer-events: none;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
}

/* ---- the hint ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Drawn only while the player is hidden (the host sets `hidden` on the document): a faint pill in
   the player's place, so a player who never learned Tab still finds the music. The host answers
   a click on it by showing the player; nothing else here takes the pointer. */

#hint {
	display: none;
	pointer-events: auto;
	position: relative;
	margin-left: 24px;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 10px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	height: 44px;
	padding: 0px 16px 0px 6px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 96);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 22);
	border-radius: 22px;
	cursor: pointer;
	transition: background-color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

#hint:hover {
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 170);
}

/* `lobby` is set by the script once the game has asked for music, the same moment the player
   starts; before that there is nothing behind the pill to open. */
body.hidden.lobby #hint {
	display: flex;
}

body.hidden #music {
	display: none;
}

#hintbadge {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 32px;
	height: 32px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 18);
	border-radius: 16px;
}

#hintbadge svg {
	display: block;
	width: 18px;
	height: 18px;
}

#hinttext {
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: bold;
	color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 200);
	white-space: nowrap;
}

/* ---- radio pill --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Dark green glass over the pitch. There is no blur and no shadow to be had -- this backend draws
   flat quads and rasterized SVG, nothing else -- so the surface is one translucent colour and the
   mock's soft edge is a 1px light border. Alpha is 0..255 in RCSS, not 0..1.

   #music is the pill's box and nothing more. The chevron and the card are positioned below it, so
   the pill stays where the document centred it whether or not the card is open. */

#music {
	pointer-events: auto;
	position: relative;
	margin-left: 24px;
	width: 330px;
}

#bar {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 12px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 330px;
	height: 62px;
	padding: 0px 12px 0px 8px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 31px;
}

/* ---- moving a panel ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Four panels are draggable, each by one handle: the pill by #bar, the replay list by #replayhead,
   the report panel by #reporthead, and #peerpaths by itself because it has no header. #replaybar
   is deliberately not one of them: it is a transient transport that only exists while a replay
   plays, #rpseek inside it already owns three drag handlers of its own, and a transport the player
   moves once and then loses under the HUD in the middle of a match is worse than one that is
   always in the same place.

   `drag: drag` is the whole mechanism. On a primary press the context walks UP from whatever was
   hit looking for an element with a drag behaviour, latches it, and sends dragstart/drag/dragend
   to it until the button comes up, wherever the pointer has gone by then. layout.lua only turns
   those positions into a left/top.

   Which means the walk decides what counts as "grabbing the handle", and `drag: block` on .control
   below is what stops it at the play glyph: block ends the search instead of passing it upwards,
   so a press on a control does what the control does and the panel stays put. #volume, #stations
   and #rpseek stop the same walk by declaring `drag: drag` themselves, which is why the rails
   inside these panels keep scrubbing and scrolling exactly as before. */
#bar {
	drag: drag;
}

/* A panel the player has moved. `moved` exists to turn OFF whichever anchoring the panel was
   built with, because the inline left/top layout.lua writes would otherwise be fighting a right,
   a bottom or a pair of auto margins that are still in force.

   The pill is the odd one: it is the only movable panel that is in FLOW, centred on the left edge
   by the body's flex box, so for it the neutralised anchoring is the flex line itself. Absolute
   also keeps it a containing block, so #card and #chevron still hang off it. */
#music.moved {
	position: absolute;
	margin-left: 0px;
}

.icon {
	font-family: Segoe MDL2 Assets;
	font-weight: normal;
}

#badge {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 46px;
	height: 46px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 18);
	border-radius: 23px;
}

#badge svg {
	display: block;
	width: 26px;
	height: 26px;
}

#now {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	overflow: hidden;
}

/* No semibold face is loaded (segoeui and segoeuib only), so the mock's semibold is bold here. */
#headline {
	display: block;
	font-size: 15px;
	font-weight: bold;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

#subline {
	display: block;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

/* ---- marquee ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Text too wide for its box scrolls instead of clipping. The box keeps its own overflow:hidden and
   holds one .scroll carrying two identical segments, the second exactly one period right of the
   first, so the wrap has nothing to see.

   Both carry an explicit pixel width written by the script, and they have to: an inline-block here
   shrink-to-fits against the space available, so a segment left to size itself comes out exactly as
   wide as the box it was supposed to overflow -- measuring it would always say "it fits". The
   script measures with scroll_width instead and states the width outright. */
.scroll {
	display: inline-block;
	white-space: nowrap;
}

.seg {
	display: inline-block;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

/* What separates the two copies: the same middle dot the hint pill uses, and its WIDTH is the gap.
   Stated here rather than left to the glyph, so the width the script declares for a segment and the
   width of what is in it agree exactly. */
.sep {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 24px;
	text-align: center;
}

/* ---- controls ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Colour and opacity transitions only: geometry stays where layout put it, so a glyph under them
   never leaves the pixel grid. An SVG carries its own fill, so its hover and press feedback is
   opacity, not colour. */
.control {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 28px;
	height: 28px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
	opacity: 0.9;
	cursor: pointer;
	transition: color opacity 0.12s quadratic-out;
	/* Ends the drag search that a press starts, rather than letting it reach the handle above:
	   pressing play inside the pill's #bar plays, and the pill does not move. See "moving a
	   panel". This costs the controls nothing that are not inside a handle. */
	drag: block;
}

/* Explicit, not content-derived: an <svg> child does not give its parent flex item a base width, so
   a control sized by content collapsed to its padding and the icons piled onto the play button. */
.control svg {
	display: block;
	width: 20px;
	height: 20px;
}

#close {
	width: 24px;
	height: 24px;
	opacity: 0.6;
}

#close svg {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}

.control:hover {
	color: #ffffff;
	opacity: 1.0;
}

.control:active {
	opacity: 0.7;
}

/* The glyph is 14px; the box around it is what a pointer has to hit. */
#chevron {
	position: absolute;
	top: 62px;
	left: 135px;
	width: 60px;
	height: 24px;
	opacity: 0.8;
}

#chevron svg {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}

/* The one lit control: the blue button, its gradient and its sheen are all inside play.svg /
   pause.svg, which the script swaps by `src`. */
.control.primary {
	width: 32px;
	height: 32px;
}

.control.primary svg {
	width: 32px;
	height: 32px;
}

/* ---- station card ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Closed, the card must neither draw nor take a click: it hangs over the game, not over the
   document, and a player would otherwise lose a strip of the lobby to an invisible panel.
   `visibility: hidden` is what removes the whole subtree from the stacking context; it is not
   inherited, but AddToStackingContext returns before descending. It is not a fade: RmlUi's opacity
   is inherited per element rather than composited, so a control carrying its own hover opacity
   would pop in at full strength while the surface was still coming up. */

#card {
	position: absolute;
	top: 84px;
	left: -10px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 350px;
	padding: 16px 16px 14px 16px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 22px;
	pointer-events: none;
	visibility: hidden;
}

#music.open #card {
	pointer-events: auto;
	visibility: visible;
}

#heading {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 16px;
}

/* The rounding is inside the image, station.svg or the station's own PNG: this backend does not
   clip an image by border-radius. The script sets `custom` when the station has its own. */
#art {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	width: 64px;
	height: 64px;
}

#art svg, #art img {
	display: block;
	width: 64px;
	height: 64px;
}

#art img, #art.custom svg {
	display: none;
}

#art.custom img {
	display: block;
}

#identity {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	overflow: hidden;
}

#name {
	display: block;
	font-size: 22px;
	font-weight: bold;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

#tagline {
	display: block;
	margin-top: 2px;
	font-size: 14px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

.section {
	margin-top: 14px;
	padding-top: 12px;
	border-top: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 26);
}

/* ---- station list, transport (legacy) -------------------------------------------------------------
   One document for both modes: the script sets `station` on #music and these rules pick the rows. */

#stations {
	display: none;
}

/* A column, capped: the card hangs below a pill centred on the screen's edge, so a list that grew
   with the station count would run off the bottom of the screen -- and nothing below the card is
   drawn, so that is not a clipped panel but a volume rail the player cannot reach. The budget is
   the shortest viewport worth supporting, 720, and the number is measured rather than reasoned
   about: script_test's "the open card fits the shortest screen worth supporting" lays the card out
   at 1280x720 and fails if it does not. Deliberately not a whole number of rows -- the one cut off
   is what says there is more below. The overflow is reached by the wheel or by dragging, both
   handled by the script, and `drag: drag` keeps the drag events coming once the pointer has left. */
#music.station #stations {
	display: block;
	max-height: 120px;
	overflow-y: auto;
	overflow-x: hidden;
	drag: drag;
}

/* One station: its art, its name over what its own clock is on right now, and its listeners. The
   border is on every row rather than only the chosen one, transparent until then: a border that
   appears on selection would move the text a pixel. */
.row {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 10px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 100%;
	padding: 5px 8px 5px 6px;
	border: 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
	border-radius: 13px;
	cursor: pointer;
	transition: background-color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

.row:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 16);
}

.row.on {
	background-color: rgba(43, 156, 242, 40);
	border: 1px #2b9cf2;
}

/* As #art: the rounding is inside the image, this backend clipping nothing to a radius. */
.rowart {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	width: 30px;
	height: 30px;
}

.rowart svg, .rowart img {
	display: block;
	width: 30px;
	height: 30px;
}

.rowtext {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	overflow: hidden;
}

/* Clipped rather than scrolled: a marquee on every row at once would be a row of moving text, and
   the chosen station's full name and track are already on the pill and in the card's heading. */
.rowname {
	display: block;
	font-size: 13px;
	font-weight: bold;
	color: #e4ebe6;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

.row.on .rowname {
	color: #ffffff;
}

.rownow {
	display: block;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

/* Absent entirely until the backend has reported counts: an empty span, not a zero. */
.rowheads {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 4px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

.rowheads .icon {
	font-size: 12px;
}

.rowcount {
	font-family: Consolas;
	font-size: 11px;
}

scrollbarhorizontal {
	height: 0px;
}

/* The list's own bar, and the only thing that says a station list is longer than the card: there
   is no shadow and no gradient to fade an edge with. `overflow-y: auto` keeps it off a list that
   fits, so a three-station radio loses no width to it. */
scrollbarvertical {
	width: 4px;
	margin-left: 6px;
}

scrollbarvertical slidertrack {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 14);
	border-radius: 2px;
}

scrollbarvertical sliderbar {
	width: 4px;
	min-height: 24px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 60);
	border-radius: 2px;
}

scrollbarvertical sliderbar:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 90);
}

/* No stepper buttons: the bar is an indicator here, and a 4px arrow is not a target. */
scrollbarvertical sliderarrowdec, scrollbarvertical sliderarrowinc {
	height: 0px;
}

/* RmlUi has no browser default style sheet: an element is `inline` unless something says otherwise,
   and an inline box ignores width and height. Flex items are blockified automatically, which is why
   the rails need no display but everything inside them does. */
#transport {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	gap: 14px;
}

#music.station #transport {
	display: none;
}

/* ---- level ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

#level {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 12px;
}

#mutebutton svg {
	width: 22px;
	height: 22px;
}

/* `drag: drag` is what makes a rail keep receiving pointer positions after the press leaves it:
   RmlUi picks the drag element by walking up from whatever was pressed -- the knob or the fill --
   and then sends every move to it until the button comes up, wherever the pointer has gone. Without
   it a scrub would stop the moment the pointer left the rail. */
#volume {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	height: 14px;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	cursor: pointer;
	drag: drag;
}

.track {
	position: relative;
	width: 100%;
	height: 4px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 56);
	border-radius: 2px;
}

.fill {
	display: block;
	height: 4px;
	background-color: #2b9cf2;
	border-radius: 2px;
}

/* Placed by its own left edge rather than a transform: a transform would take the knob off the
   pixel grid, and this one is small enough that a half pixel shows. */
.knob {
	position: absolute;
	top: -5px;
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
	margin-left: -7px;
	background-color: #ffffff;
	border-radius: 7px;
}

#loudness {
	width: 65%;
}

#loudknob {
	left: 65%;
}

/* Repeat is a legacy mode: a station's loop is the server's, not the listener's. */
.control.quiet {
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
	opacity: 1.0;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

.control.quiet .icon {
	font-size: 13px;
}

.control.quiet:hover {
	color: #ffffff;
}

/* Lit when the mode is on, which the script toggles by class rather than by writing colours. */
.control.quiet.on {
	color: #2b9cf2;
}

#music.station #repeat {
	display: none;
}

/* ---- timeline (folder mode only) --------------------------------------------------------------- */

#clock {
	margin-top: 14px;
	display: flex;
	justify-content: space-between;
	align-items: center;
	font-family: Consolas;
	font-size: 10px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

#seek {
	margin-top: 4px;
	height: 12px;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	cursor: pointer;
	drag: drag;
}

/* The radio's clock is shared: nothing to scrub, nothing to show. */
#music.station #clock, #music.station #seek {
	display: none;
}

#played {
	width: 18%;
}

#playhead {
	left: 18%;
}

/* ---- replays ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The list sits at the top right, in the lobby and only while the overlay is open: the music owns
   the left edge and the hidden state. The card follows #card's rule: closed, it neither draws nor
   takes a click. */

#replays {
	display: none;
	pointer-events: auto;
	position: absolute;
	top: 24px;
	right: 24px;
	width: 380px;
}

/* Anchored by its right edge, so that is the one property the inline left has to displace. */
#replays.moved {
	right: auto;
}

body.lobby #replays {
	display: block;
}

body.hidden #replays, body.replaywait #replays, body.replaying #replays, body.peers #replays {
	display: none;
}

#replayhead {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 10px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	height: 44px;
	padding: 0px 16px 0px 6px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 22px;
	cursor: pointer;
	drag: drag;
}

#replaybadge {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 32px;
	height: 32px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 18);
	border-radius: 16px;
}

#replaybadge svg {
	display: block;
	width: 18px;
	height: 18px;
}

#replaytitle {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	font-weight: bold;
}

#replaycount {
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

#replaychevron {
	display: block;
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}

#replaycard {
	display: block;
	margin-top: 8px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	padding: 8px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 18px;
	pointer-events: none;
	visibility: hidden;
}

#replays.open #replaycard {
	pointer-events: auto;
	visibility: visible;
}

/* Capped like #stations, so the card fits the shortest screen worth supporting (script_test). */
#replaylist {
	display: block;
	max-height: 420px;
	overflow-y: auto;
	overflow-x: hidden;
}

.replayrow {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 8px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 100%;
	padding: 6px 6px 6px 10px;
	border-radius: 12px;
	transition: background-color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

.replayrow:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 16);
}

.replayinfo {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
}

.replayteams {
	font-size: 13px;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
}

.replaymeta {
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

.replaysave.on {
	color: #f2c12b;
}

.replaydelete.confirm {
	color: #f25c54;
}

#replaynote {
	display: block;
	padding: 6px 8px 2px 10px;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

/* ---- replay bar ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Centred by equal auto margins between left and right rather than a translate, for the pixel grid
   (see .knob). Clickable while the overlay is hidden: the player holds the pointer for it. */

#replaybar {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	bottom: 28px;
	margin-left: auto;
	margin-right: auto;
	width: 780px;
	height: 56px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	padding: 0px 16px 0px 10px;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 12px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 210);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 28px;
	pointer-events: auto;
}

body.replaying #replaybar {
	display: flex;
}

#rpclock, #rpfinish {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	font-family: Consolas;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
}

#rpseek {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	height: 16px;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	cursor: pointer;
	drag: drag;
}

#rpplayed {
	width: 0%;
}

#rphead {
	left: 0%;
}

/* Where the goals are; markers only, the GOL button is what jumps. */
.rpgoal {
	position: absolute;
	top: -4px;
	width: 4px;
	height: 12px;
	margin-left: -2px;
	background-color: #f2c12b;
	border-radius: 2px;
	pointer-events: none;
}

#rpgoal, #rpspeed {
	width: 40px;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: bold;
}

#rpstatus {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	top: -22px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
}

/* ---- peer paths ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Where the replay list sits, while a network match has peers (peerpaths.lua sets `peers`): how
   each peer's game traffic travels and its round trip. It stays up with the rest of the overlay
   hidden, and it is its own drag handle, having no header to grab.

   `pointer-events: auto` is the price of that handle: the panel used to be click-through. It costs
   nothing while the overlay is down, because the router only feeds the context while the overlay
   holds the pointer (input_router::set_active), which is the same moment the player stopped
   clicking on the game. */

#peerpaths {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	top: 24px;
	right: 24px;
	padding: 6px 12px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 14px;
	pointer-events: auto;
	drag: drag;
}

/* Anchored by its right edge, like the replay list it shares the corner with. */
#peerpaths.moved {
	right: auto;
}

body.peers #peerpaths {
	display: block;
}

.peerpath {
	display: block;
	height: 20px;
	line-height: 20px;
	text-align: right;
	white-space: nowrap;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
}

.peerteam, .peermode {
	margin-right: 8px;
}

.peerteam {
	color: #ffffff;
}

.peermode, .peerms {
	font-family: Consolas;
}

.peermode {
	color: rgba(223, 231, 225, 150);
}

/* ---- replay cover -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Opaque, and last in the document so it draws over everything. It takes no click: nothing under
   it needs one, and a request that never plays expires on its own (replay_player::state). */

#replaycover {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	top: 0px;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	bottom: 0px;
	background-color: rgba(8, 16, 11, 250);
	pointer-events: none;
}

body.replaywait #replaycover {
	display: block;
}

#replaycoverbadge {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	top: 50%;
	width: 40px;
	height: 40px;
	margin: -52px auto 0px auto;
}

#replaycoverbadge svg {
	display: block;
	width: 40px;
	height: 40px;
}

#replaycovertext {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	top: 50%;
	margin-top: 4px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 16px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
}

/* ---- report -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The in-game report form. Bottom centre, because the two corners are taken: the music pill owns
   the left edge and the replay list the top right, and a card opening over either of them would
   cover the thing the player was reading when they decided to report. Centred by equal auto
   margins rather than a translate, like #replaybar, for the pixel grid.

   Compact on purpose. It is a card that hangs off a header, closed by default, and the surface a
   player sees is the 44px header and nothing else. Same glass, same radii and the same closed-card
   rule as #replaycard: closed it neither draws nor takes a click, which is `visibility: hidden`
   removing the subtree from the stacking context. */

#report {
	display: none;
	pointer-events: auto;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	bottom: 24px;
	margin-left: auto;
	margin-right: auto;
	width: 360px;
	/* Stated, not left to the header: the card is positioned by its `bottom`, which resolves
	   against THIS box, and an auto height here would make the card's place depend on the order
	   layout happened to close the two in. */
	height: 44px;
}

/* The most anchored of the four, so the most to switch off: it is centred by a left/right pair
   with auto margins between them and held to the bottom edge. All four go, and the inline left and
   top layout.lua writes are then the only things placing it. The stated height stays: the card
   above it is still positioned against this box. */
#report.moved {
	right: auto;
	bottom: auto;
	margin-left: 0px;
	margin-right: 0px;
}

/* IN A MATCH ONLY. `peers` is set by peerpaths.lua when peer_paths::read() returns rows, which it
   does only during a network match -- so this is the one class that means "a game is being played
   right now" rather than "the overlay is up". The lobby must not carry it: an owner requirement,
   and the data agrees, because outside a match the roster the panel offers is empty and the only
   report a player could file would be against a name he typed from memory.

   This is the opposite scoping to #replays, which shows in the lobby and hides on `peers`: you
   watch a finished match between matches, and you report the player you are playing against while
   you are playing him. The two panels are never on screen together, which is also why neither has
   to make room for the other. */
body.peers #report {
	display: block;
}

/* #replaybar owns this spot while a replay runs, and `hidden` is the overlay being closed. */
body.hidden #report, body.replaywait #report, body.replaying #report {
	display: none;
}

#reporthead {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	gap: 10px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	height: 44px;
	padding: 0px 16px 0px 6px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 22px;
	cursor: pointer;
	drag: drag;
}

#reportbadge {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: center;
	width: 32px;
	height: 32px;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 18);
	border-radius: 16px;
}

#reportbadge .icon {
	font-size: 16px;
	color: #dfe7e1;
}

#reporttitle {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	font-weight: bold;
}

#reportchevron {
	display: block;
	width: 14px;
	height: 14px;
}

/* 52px is the header's 44 plus the 8 the replay card leaves between itself and its own header. */
#reportcard {
	display: block;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	right: 0px;
	bottom: 52px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	padding: 10px;
	background-color: rgba(14, 30, 20, 196);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 34);
	border-radius: 18px;
	pointer-events: none;
	visibility: hidden;
}

#report.open #reportcard {
	pointer-events: auto;
	visibility: visible;
}

/* ---- kind switch and category chips -------------------------------------------------------------
   The same shape at two sizes: a lit pill for the chosen one, the station row's blue so that
   "chosen" means one thing in this document. The border is on every one of them, transparent
   until then, so lighting one does not move the text a pixel (see .row). */

#reportkind {
	display: flex;
	gap: 6px;
}

.reportseg {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	height: 28px;
	line-height: 26px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: bold;
	color: #a9b6ad;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 12);
	border: 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
	border-radius: 14px;
	cursor: pointer;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
	transition: background-color color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

.reportseg:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 24);
	color: #ffffff;
}

.reportseg.on {
	background-color: rgba(43, 156, 242, 40);
	border: 1px #2b9cf2;
	color: #ffffff;
}

#reportcats {
	display: flex;
	margin-top: 8px;
	gap: 6px;
}

.reportchip {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	height: 24px;
	line-height: 22px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 12);
	border: 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
	border-radius: 12px;
	cursor: pointer;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
	transition: background-color color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

.reportchip:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 24);
	color: #ffffff;
}

.reportchip.on {
	background-color: rgba(43, 156, 242, 40);
	border: 1px #2b9cf2;
	color: #ffffff;
}

/* ---- who is being reported ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   Capped at roughly three rows and a cut fourth, the same argument #stations makes: the card grows
   upwards off a header on the bottom edge, so a list that grew with the peer count would run off
   the top of the screen. A bug has no target, so the whole group goes away in that mode rather
   than sitting there greyed. */

#reporttargets {
	display: block;
	margin-top: 8px;
	max-height: 96px;
	overflow-y: auto;
	overflow-x: hidden;
}

#report.bug #reporttargets, #report.bug #reporttargetinput, #report.bug .reporthint {
	display: none;
}

.reportrow {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	width: 100%;
	height: 26px;
	padding: 0px 8px;
	border: 1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
	border-radius: 13px;
	font-size: 12px;
	white-space: nowrap;
	overflow: hidden;
	cursor: pointer;
	transition: background-color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

/* The name itself, because the row is a flex container and RmlUi lays out no anonymous text node
   inside one: written loose, the row drew empty. Clipping belongs here rather than on the row --
   a flex item is what a too-long team name would otherwise stretch. */
.reportteam {
	flex: 1 1 0px;
	overflow: hidden;
}

.reportrow:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 16);
}

.reportrow.on {
	background-color: rgba(43, 156, 242, 40);
	border: 1px #2b9cf2;
}

.reportempty {
	display: block;
	padding: 4px 8px;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

/* ---- the two text fields ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   RmlUi lays a text field's line out from the top of its content box, so the vertical centring is
   padding-top and not align-items: there is no line box here to centre.

   The team field is the SECOND way to name somebody, so it is not in the card until it is asked
   for: #report.typed is report.lua's answer to "is the typed name in play", which it is when the
   player pressed the line below the rows, when it already holds something, or when the roster
   named nobody to pick. Picking a row is otherwise the whole interaction, and a form with a live
   list and a live text field side by side is a form where the two can disagree. */

#reporttargetinput {
	display: none;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	margin-top: 6px;
	width: 100%;
	height: 26px;
	padding: 5px 8px 0px 8px;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #ffffff;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 12);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 26);
	border-radius: 13px;
	cursor: text;
	text-align: left;
}

#report.typed #reporttargetinput {
	display: block;
}

/* Only ever a press, never a caption: with nobody to pick, .reportempty above has already said
   the field is all there is and this line has nothing to add -- so it is laid out only while it
   is the toggle, which is exactly when report.lua marks it. */
.reporthint {
	display: none;
	margin-top: 4px;
	padding: 0px 8px;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

.reporthint.link {
	display: block;
	cursor: pointer;
}

.reporthint.link:hover {
	color: #ffffff;
}

#reporttext {
	display: block;
	box-sizing: border-box;
	margin-top: 8px;
	width: 100%;
	height: 66px;
	padding: 5px 8px;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #ffffff;
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 12);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 26);
	border-radius: 12px;
	cursor: text;
	text-align: left;
	line-height: 1.3;
}

#reporttargetinput:focus-visible, #reporttext:focus-visible {
	border: 1px #2b9cf2;
}

/* ---- counter, send, status ----------------------------------------------------------------------
   .section is the card's own separator, so the send row carries it for the same reason the music
   card's rows do. */

.reportfoot {
	display: flex;
	align-items: center;
	justify-content: space-between;
}

#reportcount {
	flex: 0 0 auto;
	font-family: Consolas;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

/* At the cap, so the player learns the text stopped growing from the counter rather than from a
   keystroke that did nothing. */
#reportcount.full {
	color: #f2c12b;
}

/* A labelled button, so it overrides .control.primary's 32px icon box. */
#reportsend {
	width: auto;
	height: 28px;
	padding: 0px 16px;
	background-color: #2b9cf2;
	border-radius: 14px;
	color: #ffffff;
	font-size: 12px;
	font-weight: bold;
	opacity: 1.0;
	transition: background-color color 0.12s quadratic-out;
}

#reportsend:hover {
	background-color: #47aaf5;
	color: #ffffff;
}

/* Greyed while one is in flight and for the 30 s afterwards. The server is what enforces the gap;
   this only stops the player spending a click on a refusal the client can already predict. */
#reportsend.off {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 20);
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

#reportsend.off:hover {
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 20);
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

#reportstatus {
	display: block;
	margin-top: 6px;
	padding: 0px 4px;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #a9b6ad;
}

#reportstatus.bad {
	color: #f25c54;
}

#reportstatus.good {
	color: #2b9cf2;
}
